Unify morphism and function application as h(x)

Morphism application is now written h(x) instead of h@(x), using the
same syntax as function application. The AST drops MorApp and FuncExpr
in favor of a single App node whose head is a term. The grammar builds
calls via ApplicationTerm (ident, member, chained app, dom/cod, or
parenthesized head) because general Term + ArgList is not LR(1).

Whether a call is a function/ctor app or a morphism app is decided
during algebraization from the head: bound vars/consts (and member
consts) are morphism heads; func/ctor names are ordinary apps; app,
dom, and cod heads are always terms. Identifier heads never introduce
variables, so a later function declaration cannot silently change a
rule, and typos stay undeclared-symbol errors.

Morphism heads use the existing mor_app machinery for typing. Mor-typed
consts may be applied as c(x); other consts keep const-called-as-
function. Wrong morphism arity is MorphismArgumentNumber. Match
patterns classify by resolution so ctor patterns and morphism-app
patterns (unification) both work; member-func pattern checks move to
the symbol-lookup pass.
